// Layered: dependencies flow downward only. Web -> Service -> Repository.
//   Pro: simple. Con: the domain depends on the persistence layer (wrong direction).

// Hexagonal: the domain is the center; ports (interfaces) define what it needs,
//   adapters implement them for HTTP, JPA, Kafka. Dependencies point INWARD.

// --- the domain (no framework imports) ---
public class OrderService {                       // core: pure Java, depends on ports only
    private final OrderRepository port;           // a PORT (interface), not a JPA repo
    public OrderService(OrderRepository port) { this.port = port; }
    public void place(Order o) {
        if (o.items().isEmpty()) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        o.markPaid();
        port.save(o);                              // infrastructure adapter fulfills this
    }
}
public interface OrderRepository { void save(Order o); }   // a port

// --- an HTTP adapter (driving side) ---
@RestController class OrderApi {
    private final OrderService svc;
    OrderApi(OrderService svc) { this.svc = svc; }
    @PostMapping("/orders") void place(@RequestBody Order o) { svc.place(o); }
}

// --- a JPA adapter (driven side) implements the port ---
@Repository class JpaOrderAdapter implements OrderRepository {
    private final OrderJpaRepository jpa;
    JpaOrderAdapter(OrderJpaRepository jpa) { this.jpa = jpa; }
    public void save(Order o) { jpa.save(OrderEntity.from(o)); }
}

```java
// Controller
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
class OrderController {
    private final OrderService orderService;

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<OrderResponse> create(
            @Valid @RequestBody CreateOrderRequest req) {
        Order order = orderService.placeOrder(req.customerId(), req.items());
        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/orders/" + order.getId()))
            .body(OrderResponse.from(order));
    }
}

// Service
@Service
class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;
    private final InventoryService inventoryService;

    @Transactional
    public Order placeOrder(String customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        inventoryService.reserve(items);
        Order order = new Order(customerId, items);
        return orderRepo.save(order);
    }
}

// Repository
interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    List<Order> findByCustomerId(String customerId);
}
```

Notice:
- Controller takes `CreateOrderRequest` DTO, not the JPA entity.
- Returns `OrderResponse` DTO via static factory.
- Service has `@Transactional` (not the controller, not the repository).
- Repository is a Spring Data interface — no implementation needed.

`@Transactional` belongs on **service methods**, not on controllers or repositories.

**Why service:**
- A user action is one transaction (place order = reserve inventory + persist order + emit event — all atomic).
- Controller handles HTTP, not business consistency.
- Repository is too granular — each method would be its own transaction; loses atomicity across multiple repo calls.

```java
@Service
class OrderService {
    @Transactional
    public Order placeOrder(...) {
        inventoryRepo.reserve(items);     // both...
        return orderRepo.save(order);     // ...in one tx
    }
}
```

**Read-only:** `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` for query methods — enables read replica routing and disables dirty checking.

**Avoid `@Transactional` on private methods or self-invocation** — Spring's proxy can't intercept; the annotation is silently ignored.
